// What a `%test` fails by.
//
//     %test
//     fn doubling_a_number_gives_twice_it() {
//         assert_eq(int(double(21)), int(42), "double(21)")
//         assert(double(0) == 0, "double(0) is nothing")
//     }
//
// `fortec <root.ft> --test -o t` builds these and an entry that runs them, and
// `./t` says which passed. A test that fails no assertion passed; one that
// failed any did not, and the runner exits non-zero so that whatever ran it
// knows.
//
// **A failed assertion does not stop the test.** It says what went wrong,
// counts itself, and returns, so the rest of the body runs on values the test
// has just said are wrong. Nothing in the language unwinds -- there is no
// panic and no exception, and a callee cannot return on its caller's behalf --
// so the alternatives were taking the whole process down on the first failure,
// which abandons every test after it, or jumping out over frames whose drops
// are written and would not run. `runtime/src/test.rs` is the other half of
// this file and argues it out at greater length.
//
// So a test that checks a length and then reads that many elements will fail
// its assertion and then run off the end of something. Where the rest of a test
// cannot survive the failure, write the `return`:
//
//     %test
//     fn a_vector_holds_what_was_put_in_it() {
//         var v: Vec<i64> = empty()
//         push(*v, 7)
//         assert_eq(&len(&v), &1, "one thing was pushed")
//         if len(&v) != 1 { return }
//         assert_eq(&at(&v, 0), &7, "and it is the thing")
//     }

import fmt::{Arg, Show};

%symbol("__rt_assert")
fn said(ok: i64, why: str): null;

// One symbol for both sides of the question, `same` being which way round it
// was asked -- the same shape `print` uses for its two flags.
%symbol("__rt_assert_cmp")
fn said_cmp(same: i64, a: Arg, b: Arg, why: str): null;

// ---- Asserting ---------------------------------------------------------------

// The plain one. `why` is what the reader is told when it does not hold, so it
// says what was meant to be true rather than what was written -- the expression
// is in the file and the intention is not.
pub fn assert(ok: bool, why: str) {
    said(ok as i64, why)
}

// The two-sided ones, which are worth having because they can say what the two
// sides *were*: `assert(a == b, ...)` can only ever report that they differed.
//
// Each side is asked what it looks like, exactly as `println` asks -- so a
// value reads in a failure the way it reads in a line of output, and neither
// place needs a word from the caller about which kind it is. They used to take
// an `Arg` apiece and be handed `int(a)` and `text(b)`, which is the wrapping
// `fmt` explains and no longer needs.
//
// What crosses to the runtime is still an `Arg`, because that is what the
// runtime can read; what a caller writes is a reference to the value.
//
// **A bound and not a `&dyn Show`**, which is the one decision here worth
// writing down. Both sides being one `T` says what an equality assert means --
// a number is never equal to a piece of text, and `assert_eq(&5, &"5")` is a
// question nobody meant to ask, so it is refused rather than answered.
//
// It also settles what a bare literal is. `assert_eq(&count, &10, ..)` is the
// commonest shape there is, and a `10` with no suffix is a hole: as a
// `&dyn Show` there is nothing anywhere to fill it from and it is refused, and
// as a `&T` beside `&count` it is whatever `count` is. So the bound buys the
// spelling the callers want as well as the rule.
pub fn assert_eq<T: Show>(a: &T, b: &T, why: str) {
    said_cmp(1, a.show(), b.show(), why)
}

pub fn assert_ne<T: Show>(a: &T, b: &T, why: str) {
    said_cmp(0, a.show(), b.show(), why)
}
